# Repository root .gitattributes, added per docs/08-devops/coding-standards.md's own
# Static Analysis and Linting table naming .editorconfig as the formatting authority --
# that file's own [*] section sets `end_of_line = lf` for every file in this
# repository, with no override for any extension. Without this file, a checkout on a
# machine whose local `core.autocrlf` setting (or a git client's own default, e.g.
# Git for Windows) converts LF to CRLF on checkout silently drifts the working tree
# away from that policy -- every file `dotnet format` or an editor then touches shows
# as "modified" in git status/diff with zero actual content change, which is exactly
# the noise that showed up across the Configuration, Logging, Identity, and Event
# Framework pull requests. This file makes the LF policy structural (git enforces it on
# every checkout, on every machine) rather than something each developer's own local
# git configuration has to happen to already agree with -- the same "prefer structure
# over discipline" principle the rest of this repository already follows.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect genuinely binary files by content and leave them
# alone; the explicit `binary` declarations below are the same safety net every
# `.gitattributes` file carries for the file types most likely to be added to this
# repository's own docs/ and future frontend/ trees, even though none are tracked yet.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
